{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Take.</b> He does not ask of you, when you go to seek His favor, wealth, or burnt offerings — only words, that you confess, in the manner of \"and the goat shall bear upon it\" (Leviticus 16:22). Rabbi Marinus said: כל תשא עון (Hosea 14:3) is the reverse of כל עון תשא. The correct reading: כל אשר תשא עונינו — נשוב." "<b>And accept what is good.</b> Either a burnt offering, or the good speech that we are able to say before You — the meaning being, to confess. Rabbi Joseph said: a minimum of good deeds." "<b>And let us render.</b> The peace-offerings of cattle, like \"for You strike the cheek of Your enemies\" (1 Samuel 2:10?), whose meaning is: a striking of the cheek, or lacking a כ\"ף. And he mentioned cattle, for they are great and precious in burnt offering, and this is what you shall say."], :book :hosea, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "The reading כל אשר תשא עונינו appears to reflect a proposed textual correction to the verse; the second clause נשוב ('we will return') seems to complete the phrase but the exact relationship is uncertain.", 2 "The verse cited as כי הכת את אויבי לחי does not match 1 Samuel 2:10 exactly; the reference is uncertain and may be corrupt. The argument about a missing כ\"ף (i.e., reading לחי as ל-לחי, 'to the cheek') is preserved as-is."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Hosea -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/daat/olam_hatanah/mefaresh.asp?book=15&perek=1&mefaresh=ezra"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:51:19.390364544Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}